Title: The Call of the Wild

Credit: Written by

Author: Jack London

Source: Adapted from book

Draft date: 7/31/2026

FADE IN:

EXT. JUDGE MILLER'S ESTATE - DAY

Period live-action. A vast, sunlit estate in California: lawns, orchards, stables, and a cement swimming tank. BUCK, a massive 140-pound St. Bernard-Scotch shepherd with long golden-brown fur, a white chest, and a broad wolfish muzzle, surveys it all like a king.

(SOUND: fountain water, children laughing, distant kennel dogs barking.)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Buck did not read the newspapers, or he would have known that trouble was brewing for every tide-water dog, strong of muscle and with warm, long hair, from Puget Sound to San Diego.

Buck plunges into the tank, carries a child on his back, then settles beside JUDGE MILLER'S chair before the library fire.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
And over this great demesne Buck ruled. He was king over all creeping, crawling, flying things of Judge Miller's place, humans included.

EXT. JUDGE MILLER'S ORCHARD - NIGHT

MANUEL, a nervous gardener's helper in work clothes, leads Buck through the dark orchard. Buck follows trustingly, collar gleaming.

(SOUND: night insects, leaves brushing together, Buck's collar softly jingling.)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
In the fall of 1897, men were rushing into the frozen North because they had found a yellow metal. Manuel loved to play Chinese lottery, and his wages did not lap over the needs of a wife and numerous progeny.

Manuel glances toward the distant house, then hurries Buck onward.

EXT. COLLEGE PARK FLAG STATION - NIGHT

A lonely station lamp burns beside the tracks. A SOLITARY KIDNAPPER waits with a coil of stout rope. Money changes hands between him and Manuel.

(SOUND: approaching train, coins chinking, telegraph wire humming.)

KIDNAPPER
You might wrap up the goods before you deliver 'm.

Manuel doubles the rope around Buck's neck beneath his brass collar.

MANUEL
Twist it, an' you'll choke 'm plentee.

The kidnapper takes the rope. Buck's growl becomes a roar as the rope tightens.

(SOUND: rope fibers creaking, Buck choking and thrashing.)

Buck launches at the kidnapper. The man grapples him, throws him down, and hauls until Buck's eyes glaze.

The train arrives. The men drag Buck into the baggage car.

(SOUND: locomotive whistle blasting, train brakes shrieking.)

INT. TRAIN BAGGAGE CAR - NIGHT

Buck lies in a battered crate, throat raw, tongue swollen. The train jolts through darkness.

(SOUND: wheels hammering the rails, iron couplings clanging.)

Buck wakes and springs at the KIDNAPPER. His jaws close on the man's hand.

(SOUND: a strangled cry, teeth clamping, the train's whistle.)

KIDNAPPER
Yep, has fits. I'm takin' 'm up for the boss to 'Frisco. A crack dog-doctor there thinks that he can cure 'm.

Buck collapses back into the crate as the train races north.

INT. SAN FRANCISCO WATERFRONT SHED - NIGHT

A cramped shed behind a saloon. Buck, bruised and furious, is dragged from the crate by the KIDNAPPER and the SALOON KEEPER. A bloody handkerchief wraps the kidnapper's hand.

(SOUND: shed door rattling, harbor chains knocking, a distant foghorn.)

SALOON KEEPER
How much did the other mug get?

KIDNAPPER
A hundred. Wouldn't take a sou less, so help me.

He looks at the torn trousers and mangled hand.

KIDNAPPER
All I get is fifty for it, an' I wouldn't do it over for a thousand, cold cash.

The men file away Buck's brass collar, choke him loose, and fling him into a cage-like crate.

(SOUND: metal file scraping, Buck's low growl, a crate door slamming.)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
For two days and nights he neither ate nor drank. He accumulated a fund of wrath that boded ill for whoever first fell foul of him.

EXT. SEATTLE DOG-BREAKER'S YARD - DAY

A small, high-walled yard. Buck, still huge and long-furred but red-eyed and gaunt from thirst, is carried in a splintered crate. A STOUT DOG-BREAKER in a sagging red sweater signs the delivery book and lifts a hatchet and club.

(SOUND: wagon wheels stopping, crate boards creaking, men muttering above the wall.)

RED SWEATER
You ain't going to take him out now?

He drives the hatchet into the crate.

RED SWEATER
Now, you red-eyed devil.

Buck tears through the opening and launches at him.

(SOUND: splintering wood, Buck's furious bark, the club smashing into bone.)

Buck charges again. Again the club stops him and hurls him into the mud.

(SOUND: a second, heavier club impact, men gasping from the wall.)

After a final blow, Buck lies senseless. The red sweater kneels, sets down the club, and brings water and raw meat.

RED SWEATER
You've learned your place, and I know mine. Be a good dog and all'll go well. Be a bad dog, and I'll whale the stuffin' outa you. Understand?

Buck drinks. He eats from the man's hand.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
That club was a revelation. It was his introduction to the reign of primitive law.

PERRAULT, a little wizened French-Canadian courier, enters the yard with money in hand.

PERRAULT
Sacredam! Dat one dam bully dog! Eh? How moch?

RED SWEATER
Three hundred, and a present at that.

Money passes. Buck is led away.

INT. NARWHAL - TWEEN-DECKS - DAY

The ship's dark hold trembles around Buck, CURLY, a friendly Newfoundland, SPITZ, a large snow-white husky with a sly smile, and DAVE, a morose, battle-worn sled dog who sleeps through the ship's pitching.

(SOUND: propeller throbbing, timbers groaning, chains shifting.)

FRANCOIS, a black-faced French-Canadian half-breed with a whip at his belt, leashes the dogs. Spitz steals a bone from Buck's ration.

Buck springs. Francois's whip reaches Spitz first.

(SOUND: whip cutting the air, Spitz yelping, ship rolling hard.)

Buck retrieves the bone. He watches Francois with wary respect.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Perrault and Francois were fair men, calm and impartial in administering justice, and too wise in the way of dogs to be fooled by dogs.

Dave yawns, turns his back on the others, and sleeps.

EXT. NARWHAL - DECK - DAY

Perrault brings the dogs onto the cold deck. Snow falls from a gray sky. Buck's paws sink into the white mush.

(SOUND: wind over the deck, rigging clattering, sailors laughing.)

Buck jerks back, sniffs the snow, and licks it. It burns his tongue and vanishes.

(SOUND: a wet hiss, then laughter rolling across the deck.)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
It bit like fire, and the next instant was gone. This puzzled him. It was his first snow.

EXT. DYEA BEACH CAMP - DAY

A raw beach camp beneath mountains. Buck, long-furred and still powerful, stands beside Curly as a husky the size of a wolf approaches.

(SOUND: wind over snow, harness bells, camp dogs growling.)

Curly makes a friendly advance.

The husky leaps in, tears her face from eye to jaw, and springs away.

(SOUND: metallic clip of teeth, Curly's cry.)

Curly rushes again. The husky knocks her down. Thirty or forty huskies close in, silent and intent.

Curly is buried beneath the bristling mass.

(SOUND: snarling pack, Curly screaming, men shouting.)

Francois charges in with an axe as three men beat the dogs away. Curly lies limp and bloody in the trampled snow. Spitz stands apart, tongue lolling in what looks like laughter.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
No fair play. Once down, that was the end of you. From that moment Buck hated Spitz with a bitter and deathless hatred.

EXT. DYEA FOREST TRAIL - DAY

Buck, his long fur bright against the snow, wears a new harness beside Dave and Spitz. SOL-LEKS, an old, one-eyed husky with a scarred face, joins the line.

(SOUND: harness buckles snapping, sled runners grinding, whip cracking.)

Francois leans into the gee-pole.

FRANCOIS
Mush!

The team surges. Dave nips Buck into place. Spitz jerks the lead. Buck learns the bends, the commands, and the loaded sled racing downhill behind him.

PERRAULT
T'ree vair' good dogs. Dat Buck, heem pool lak hell.

Buck pulls harder, his breath smoking in the cold.

EXT. DYEA BEACH CAMP - NIGHT

A glowing tent sits in a white wilderness. Buck tries to enter and is driven back by curses and flying cooking utensils.

(SOUND: wind biting through canvas, pans clattering, dogs barking in the dark.)

He circles the tent, shivering, then sinks through a soft patch of snow. BILLEE, a gentle husky, wriggles from a warm hole beneath him and licks Buck's face.

Buck watches, understands, and digs his own sleeping hole.

(SOUND: Buck's claws scraping snow, wind fading around the hollow.)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Another lesson. Buck confidently selected a spot, dug a hole for himself, and in a trice the heat from his body filled the confined space.

EXT. YUKON TRAIL - DAY

Buck, now leaner but still massive, pulls with the team through white valleys and over hard blue ice. Days and nights compress into a rhythm of harness, trail, fish, and snow.

(SOUND: runners rasping, bells jingling, dogs panting in unison.)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Day after day, for days unending, Buck toiled in the traces. The pound and a half of sun-dried salmon, which was his ration for each day, seemed to go nowhere.

Pike, a sly husky, steals a slice of bacon. The next day Buck steals the whole chunk while Perrault's back is turned. DUB, an awkward blunderer, is punished for it.

(SOUND: a distant lash, dogs snarling over food.)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
In the Northland, under the law of club and fang, whoso took such things into account was a fool.

At night, Buck lifts his muzzle toward the stars.

(SOUND: Buck's long wolf howl joining the distant team.)

EXT. LAKE LE BARGE CAMP - NIGHT

A bleak camp on the frozen lake. Driving snow lashes a rock wall. Buck's nest glows faintly beside a starving fire.

(SOUND: wind cutting like a knife, ice cracking beneath the fire.)

Buck returns from his ration and finds Spitz occupying his nest. Buck's hair rises. He springs.

FRANCOIS
Gif it to heem, by Gar! Gif it to heem, the dirty t'eef!

Buck and Spitz circle, teeth flashing—until starving huskies pour from the darkness.

(SOUND: a sudden eruption of howls, clubs striking ribs, snarling bodies crashing through camp.)

The gaunt invaders tear into the food and then the team. Buck kills one at the throat; Spitz bites Buck from the side. The dogs break and flee across the ice.

(SOUND: frantic paws on ice, men shouting, canvas ripping, the fire collapsing.)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
They were mere skeletons, draped loosely in draggled hides, but hunger made them terrifying, irresistible.

EXT. FROZEN CREEK - NIGHT

Moonlight turns the creek into a silver track. A snowshoe rabbit races ahead of Buck and a pack of sixty dogs.

(SOUND: paws pounding crusted snow, the rabbit's quick breath, the pack's rising cry.)

Spitz cuts across the bend, catches the rabbit in midair, and breaks its back.

The pack erupts. Buck drives into Spitz shoulder-first.

(SOUND: the rabbit's shriek, a roar of dogs, bodies rolling in powder.)

The two rivals rise and circle. The silent pack forms a ring around them.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Buck knew it. The time had come. It was to the death.

Spitz slashes Buck's shoulder. Buck feints for the throat, then sweeps low and catches Spitz's foreleg.

(SOUND: bone crunching, the pack going suddenly silent.)

Spitz staggers on three legs. Buck breaks the other foreleg and drives him into the snow.

(SOUND: a final impact, snarling cut short.)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Mercy was a thing reserved for gentler climes. Buck stood and looked on, the successful champion, the dominant primordial beast.

EXT. YUKON TRAIL CAMP - DAWN

Buck, scarred across the muzzle and shoulders, stands at the front of the harness line. Francois brings SOL-LEKS forward to lead.

(SOUND: dawn wind, dogs whining, harness rings clinking.)

Buck displaces Sol-leks and plants himself in the lead position.

FRANCOIS
Go 'way, Chook!

Buck refuses. Francois raises a heavy club. Buck retreats just beyond its reach, watching the weapon.

PERRAULT
T'row down de club.

Francois lowers it. Sol-leks returns to his old place. Buck trots in, swings to the front, and accepts the traces.

(SOUND: runners breaking free, bells snapping into rhythm.)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
At a bound Buck took up the duties of leadership; and where judgment was required, and quick thinking and quick acting, he showed himself the superior even of Spitz.

The team surges down the trail as one dog.

EXT. YUKON MAIL TRAIL - DAY

Months later. Buck, long-furred but gaunt and footsore, leads a heavy mail train through soft snow. DAVE, once proud in the traces, stumbles and cries out.

(SOUND: labored breathing, runners dragging, a weak dog crying.)

The mail driver removes Dave from the harness. Dave attacks Sol-leks, bites through his traces, and returns to his rightful place before the sled.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The pride of trace and trail was his. Sick unto death, he could not bear that another dog should do his work.

Dave pulls. He falls, rises, falls again. At the next camp he lies by the fire, too weak to travel.

(SOUND: a revolver shot behind the river trees.)

The train goes on. Buck turns his head toward the sound.

(SOUND: bells and whips resume, receding into the distance.)

EXT. SKAGUAY MAIN STREET - DAY

Thirty days later. Buck, reduced to 115 pounds, with matted fur and raw feet, limps into Skaguay at the head of the exhausted team.

(SOUND: crowded street, sled bells, dogs coughing and panting.)

CHARLES, middle-aged and watery-eyed beneath a fierce mustache, bargains with a government agent. HAL, barely twenty, wears a cartridge-bristling belt and carries a revolver. MERCEDES, pretty and soft-faced, clings to both men.

The new owners pile an absurd load onto the sled.

HAL
Mush on there!

The dogs strain. The frozen runners do not move.

HAL raises his whip.

MERCEDES
Oh, Hal, you mustn't.

She wrenches away the whip. Hal takes it back.

HAL
They're lazy, I tell you. You've got to whip them to get anything out of them.

The runners break loose. The overloaded sled lurches downhill, overturns, and spills its cargo. Hal is dragged beneath it as the dogs run on.

(SOUND: whip cracking, wood splintering, sled crashing, townspeople shouting.)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The dogs were angry because of the ill treatment they had received and the unjust load.

EXT. SKAGUAY CAMP - EVENING

The new camp is a disorderly wreck. Blankets, dishes, canned goods, clothes, and tent canvas lie scattered in the snow. Mercedes weeps as strangers remove half the load.

(SOUND: tin dishes clattering, canvas snapping, Mercedes sobbing.)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The inexorable elimination of the superfluous began. Yet the outfit was still formidable, and Charles and Hal bought more dogs until they had fourteen.

Pointer dogs and mongrels stand bewildered beside the old huskies. Buck watches Charles and Hal calculate rations over a pencil and scrap of paper.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
They had worked the trip out with a pencil. In the nature of Arctic travel, there was a reason why fourteen dogs should not drag one sled: one sled could not carry the food for fourteen dogs.

EXT. WHITE RIVER TRAIL - DAY

Spring sunlight glitters on rotten snow. Buck, skeletal beneath his dull, blood-matted coat, staggers at the head of seven remaining dogs. Mercedes rides the overloaded sled.

(SOUND: thawing water beneath the ice, weak harness bells, dogs' paws dragging.)

The fish sacks hang nearly empty. The dogs chew strips of frozen horse-hide.

Billee collapses in the traces. Hal takes up an axe.

(SOUND: axe blade striking, Mercedes crying out.)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
As it was with Buck, so was it with his mates. They were perambulating skeletons.

Only Buck, Joe, Pike, Sol-leks, and Teek remain. Buck falls and refuses to rise.

The trail opens onto a dangerous stretch of thin ice.

EXT. JOHN THORNTON'S CAMP - DAY

At the mouth of White River, JOHN THORNTON, a weathered woodsman in a wool coat, whittles a birch axe-handle. Buck lies before him, huge-boned but nearly lifeless.

(SOUND: knife shaving wood, spring water running under ice.)

HAL yanks the whip from his belt.

HAL
Get up there, Buck! Hi! Get up there! Mush on!

The whip lashes Buck. He does not move.

(SOUND: whip cutting air, leather striking fur, Thornton's breathing tightening.)

Hal exchanges the whip for a club. Thornton rises.

THORNTON
And they told you true. The bottom's likely to drop out at any moment. I wouldn't risk my carcass on that ice for all the gold in Alaska.

HAL
That's because you're not a fool, I suppose. All the same, we'll go on to Dawson.

Hal strikes Buck again. Thornton springs upon him and throws him backward.

(SOUND: a body hitting snow, Mercedes screaming.)

THORNTON
If you strike that dog again, I'll kill you.

HAL
It's my dog. Get out of my way, or I'll fix you.

Hal draws his knife. Thornton knocks it loose with the axe-handle, picks it up, and cuts Buck's traces.

(SOUND: metal ringing across ice, leather straps snapping.)

The sled departs. A hundred yards away, the ice gives way. Dogs, sled, and humans vanish into a yawning hole.

(SOUND: ice cracking like gunfire, Mercedes's distant scream, then a vast plunge.)

Thornton kneels beside Buck and searches his bruised body.

THORNTON
You poor devil.

Buck licks his hand.

EXT. JOHN THORNTON'S CAMP - DAY

Weeks later. Buck is restored: broad-chested, long-furred, scarred around the muzzle, with a white blaze down his chest. SKEET, a small Irish setter, cleans his wounds. NIG, a huge black half-bloodhound, nudges him into play.

(SOUND: birdsong, river current, quiet fire popping.)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Love, genuine passionate love, that was feverish and burning, that was adoration, it had taken John Thornton to arouse.

Thornton takes Buck's head between his hands and shakes him roughly.

THORNTON
God! You can all but speak!

Buck seizes Thornton's hand gently but fiercely between his jaws. Thornton laughs and lets him hold it.

(SOUND: Buck's happy growl, Thornton's rough laughter.)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Thornton alone held him. The rest of mankind was as nothing.

EXT. FORTY-MILE CREEK RAPIDS - DAY

Later that fall. Thornton stands in a narrow poling boat while HANS and PETE, his weathered partners in fur coats, work ropes along the bank. Buck runs beside the water, eyes fixed on his master.

(SOUND: rapids roaring, rope tightening around trees, Thornton shouting directions.)

The boat flips. Thornton is thrown into the current and swept toward the rocks.

Buck leaps in.

(SOUND: a violent splash, rushing water swallowing the shore.)

THORNTON
Go, Buck! Go!

Buck reaches Thornton, but the rope jerks him under. Hans and Pete drag him ashore, half drowned.

(SOUND: rope snapping taut, Buck choking, men pounding water from his lungs.)

Thornton's voice carries faintly from the rapids. Buck rises and launches again, this time striking straight toward him. Thornton grabs Buck's neck.

(SOUND: current roaring, rope paying out, both bodies dragged beneath the water.)

Hans and Pete haul them to shore. Thornton wakes beside Buck's limp body.

THORNTON
That settles it. We camp right here.

Skeet licks Buck's face as he breathes.

INT. ELDORADO SALOON - NIGHT

A crowded Dawson saloon. Gold dust sacks sit on the bar. JOHN THORNTON, Hans, and Pete face MATTHEWSON, a broad, boastful mine owner.

(SOUND: saloon voices, glasses knocking, a piano stumbling in the background.)

MATTHEWSON
My dog can start a sled with seven hundred pounds.

THORNTON
Pooh! Pooh! Buck can start a thousand pounds.

MATTHEWSON
And break it out? And walk off with it for a hundred yards?

THORNTON
And break it out, and walk off with it for a hundred yards.

Matthewson slams a sausage-sized sack of gold dust onto the bar.

(SOUND: gold thudding on wood; the room falling silent.)

MATTHEWSON
I've got a thousand dollars that says he can't. And there it is.

Thornton looks to JIM O'BRIEN.

THORNTON
Can you lend me a thousand?

JIM O'BRIEN
Sure. Though it's little faith I'm having, John, that the beast can do the trick.

EXT. ELDORADO SALOON - DAY

A crowd surrounds a sled loaded with twenty fifty-pound sacks of flour. Buck, restored to 150 pounds, shines with health; his long fur lifts over a massive chest.

(SOUND: sixty-below-zero wind, men stamping boots, gamblers calling odds.)

SKOOKUM KING
I offer you eight hundred for him, sir. Eight hundred just as he stands.

Thornton shakes his head and kneels beside Buck, cheek against cheek.

THORNTON
As you love me, Buck. As you love me.

Buck closes his jaws around Thornton's mittened hand, then releases it slowly.

Thornton steps back.

THORNTON
Now, Buck.

Buck tightens the traces.

THORNTON
Gee!

Buck swings right. The frozen runners crack.

(SOUND: crisp ice splitting beneath the sled.)

THORNTON
Haw!

Buck swings left. The runners snap free.

(SOUND: wood and ice grinding, the crowd holding its breath.)

THORNTON
Now, MUSH!

Buck throws his whole body forward. The sled jerks, moves, and then rolls steadily across the hard-packed snow.

(SOUND: claws scraping, runners surging, a roar rising from hundreds of men.)

Buck passes the firewood marker. The crowd explodes. Thornton falls on his knees beside him, shaking him and cursing softly.

SKOOKUM KING
I'll give you a thousand for him, sir—a thousand, sir—twelve hundred, sir.

THORNTON
No, sir. You can go to hell, sir. It's the best I can do for you, sir.

EXT. LOST MINE VALLEY - DAY

Months later. Thornton, Hans, Pete, Buck, and a small team stand in a broad valley where gold lies like yellow butter in the washing pan. Moose-hide sacks of dust pile outside a rough spruce-bough lodge.

(SOUND: pickaxes striking frozen earth, sluice water running, distant ravens.)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
They sought no farther. Each day they worked earned them thousands of dollars in clean dust and nuggets, and they worked every day.

Buck watches the men heap gold like firewood, then turns toward the dark forest beyond the valley.

EXT. LOST MINE CAMP - NIGHT

Buck lies by the fire. Gold sacks loom behind him. In the flames appears the vision of a short-legged, long-armed hairy man, crouched beside another ancient fire, clutching a stone-headed stick.

(SOUND: fire popping, branches breaking far beyond the circle of light.)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The call was sounding in the depths of the forest. Mysteriously thrilling and luring, it compelled Buck to turn his back upon the fire and plunge into the forest.

From the darkness comes a long, unfamiliar howl.

(SOUND: the howl rising beyond the trees, answered faintly from far away.)

Buck lifts his head. His mane bristles.

EXT. FOREST CLEARING - NIGHT

Buck, broad and powerful with a white chest and wolfish muzzle, stalks into a moonlit clearing. A long, lean WILD WOLF stands on his haunches, nose pointed to the sky.

(SOUND: the wild wolf's howl fading into tense silence.)

The wolf flees. Buck chases him through trees and into a blind creek channel. The wolf whirls, snarling.

Buck circles without attacking, offering his nose. The wolf darts away again.

(SOUND: paws striking wet earth, branches snapping, warning snarls.)

At last they touch noses. The wolf relaxes and lopes onward. Buck runs beside him over the divide.

At a clear stream, Buck stops. The wolf waits, whining softly.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Buck remembered John Thornton.

Buck turns back toward camp. The wolf points his nose to the sky and howls as Buck disappears.

(SOUND: the wild wolf's mournful howl thinning into the distance.)

EXT. LOST MINE CAMP - DAY

Buck bursts into camp and knocks Thornton into the blankets, licking his face and biting his hand in ecstatic affection.

(SOUND: Thornton laughing, Buck's ecstatic growl, dogs barking around the fire.)

THORNTON
You are playing the general tom-fool.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
For two days and nights Buck never left camp. But after two days the call in the forest began to sound more imperiously than ever.

Buck watches Thornton sleep. Then he lifts his head toward the trees and slips away.

EXT. HIGH COUNTRY DIVIDE - DAY

Buck, now nearly indistinguishable from a gigantic wolf except for his white chest and brown muzzle, stalks a great wounded moose bull with fourteen-point antlers.

(SOUND: moose bellowing, brush breaking, Buck's low warning bark.)

He cuts the bull from the herd, darts clear of its hooves, and drives it away from the younger moose. The herd abandons the wounded animal.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
There is a patience of the wild—dogged, tireless, persistent as life itself—that belonged to Buck.

Four days and nights pass in flashes of hooves, snow, thirst, and pursuit. The bull finally collapses.

(SOUND: the moose's final bellow, then only Buck's breath.)

Buck eats, sleeps, and turns his face toward camp.

EXT. LOST MINE CAMP - DUSK

Buck approaches the camp silently, every nerve tense. A fresh trail leads through the trees.

(SOUND: birds gone silent, wind whispering through spruce.)

He finds NIG dead beneath a tree, an arrow through his body. A hundred yards farther, HANS lies feathered with arrows. The spruce-bough lodge is wrecked. YEEHAT HUNTERS dance around the ruins, led by the YEEHAT CHIEF.

Buck sees the place where Thornton lived.

(SOUND: Yeehat chanting, fire crackling, Buck's growl swelling into a roar.)

Buck attacks. He tears through the hunters in a blur of fur and teeth. Arrows fly; hunters strike one another in panic. The survivors flee into the forest.

(SOUND: screams, arrows cutting air, running feet, then sudden silence.)

Buck returns to the camp. PETE lies dead in his blankets. SKEET lies beside a muddy pool, faithful to the end.

Buck follows Thornton's scent into the water. No trace leads away.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
John Thornton was dead. It left a great void in Buck, somewhat akin to hunger, but a void which food could not fill.

EXT. LOST MINE VALLEY - NIGHT

A full moon bathes the valley. Buck stands motionless beside the pool, enormous and silvered, his white chest bright beneath his long fur.

(SOUND: distant yelps approaching through the forest, water lapping at the bank.)

A pack of wolves pours into the clearing. The boldest leaps. Buck strikes, breaking its neck.

(SOUND: bone cracking, the pack recoiling.)

Three more wolves rush him and retreat bleeding. The whole pack surges forward. Buck backs into a gravel bank, protected on three sides.

(SOUND: snarling bodies colliding, claws scraping stone, wolves panting.)

After half an hour, the pack withdraws. A long, lean gray wolf advances. Buck recognizes the WILD BROTHER. They touch noses.

An OLD WOLF, gaunt and battle-scarred, approaches. He raises his muzzle and howls.

(SOUND: the old wolf's long howl, the pack answering in a rising chorus.)

Buck sits and joins them.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
John Thornton was dead. The last tie was broken. Man and the claims of man no longer bound him.

Buck steps from the bank. The pack crowds around him, then races into the forest. Buck runs beside the wild brother.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The Yeehats tell of a Ghost Dog that runs at the head of the pack—a great, gloriously coated wolf, like and yet unlike all other wolves.

Seasons pass. In summer, Buck returns alone to the overgrown camp where yellow dust runs from rotted moose-hide sacks into the grass.

(SOUND: one long, mournful howl carrying across the valley.)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
And when the long winter nights come on, he may be seen running at the head of the pack through the pale moonlight or glimmering borealis, his great throat a-bellow as he sings a song of the younger world.

Buck disappears into the timber with the pack, howling beneath the northern lights.

FADE OUT.

THE END
